Nickelodeon's Doug is still a major  source of nostalgia for early  Millennials who grew up in the splat TV  era The Diary style cartoon series  centered on the life of a boy in  small-town bluffington as he grappled  with the typical issues of youth from  navigating an unrequited infatuation to  concealing a superhero alter ego Doug  funnies life was both relatable and  charming to watch but what you may not  know about the series is how it became  so beloved in the first place and how it  hit a few major speed bumps along the  way here's a look back at some of the  things you might not know about Doug the  life of Brian it's hard to imagine Doug  as being about anyone except well Doug  but creator Jim Jenkins originally had  another name in mind  in 2012 Jenkins told split cider that  since the character was supposed to be a  very any guy look he wanted to name him  something completely blase Jenkins  explained at the beginning I was calling  him Brian it was sort of just a generic  name I thought that Doug had that  feeling and that sound about it it was a  conscious choice because I wanted him to  be this very neutral middle-of-the-road  guy Doug's middle name Yancey also had  some significance for Jenkins as it was  shared by his own grandfather  Doug's embarrassment over it stemmed  from a very real place as he had a  childhood friend who also hated his own  name growing up much to Jenkins  amusement ad man Jenkins Doug doodles  originally existed as a sort of  alter-ego for the artist who said he  desperately needed an outlet from the  real world where problems are a lot more  complicated than they are for the  residents of his pseudo utopia  bluffington Jenkins once explained of  the character's origin I like to doodle  and Doug was just one of those guys that  kept doodling over and over mercial  Watchers might even remember a very  early version of Doug bouncing around on  a pogo stick in his signature vest for a  1989 Florida grapefruit juice commercial  and he and his prized pooch porkchop  were later seen in 1990 USA Network  commercial bluffington is real  eventually Jenkins collected his  drawings into a book called Doug got a  new pair of shoes and sent a copy to a  Nickelodeon executive which led to the  network ordering an eight-minute pilot  episode called Doug Kent  Jenkins immediately went to work  creating his own production studio to  make it happen as he began developing  other characters for the series  Jenkins again drew inspiration from his  own life and the people he knew it's  kind of like how I remember childhood  Doug's love interest Patti mayonnaise  for example was based on Jenkins own  childhood crush supporting characters  Roger Klotz and Doug's best friend  Skeeter Valentine were also based on  people Jenkins grew up with no  instruments needed you probably know  that mega famous voice actor Billy West  also known for voicing the title  characters for Nickelodeon's popular  cartoon series the Ren &amp; Stimpy show was  cast to portray Doug who's a painfully  shy 11 and a half year old  however West wasn't the only voice actor  to make a major impact on the show actor  Fred Newman was brought on to voice some  of the zanier sounding characters  including porkchop Skeeter and mr. dink  whatever  I'm dink bud dink you can call me mr.  dink Newman even ended up creating that  earworm of a theme song using the  simplest of tools of that catchy intro  Newman explained that wasn't Doug's  voice that was actually my voice we  wanted to use as much as we could  household objects things that a kid  could have make it sound too innocent  that's right Newman relied on his unique  beatboxing and honking skills to create  a lot of the sounds heard throughout the  catchy intro  [Music]  the rainbow rebuke the title character  of Doug and his family were drawn to be  very obviously Caucasian but many other  characters in the series boasted rather  unusual skin colors from skeeters teal  complexion to Rodgers lime-green tone to  Beebe Bluffs purplish appearance however  Jenkins insists he wasn't trying to make  any major socio-political statements  with his colorful cast of characters  Jenkins told huff posts of the  controversy over his main character  skintone people are certain that Skeeter  is an african-american guy I'm like well  he's blue if that's how you see him  that's great it's not a bad thing but I  never planned it Skeeter is blue and  he's Doug's friend a networked angle  following its original 52 episode run  from 1991 to 1994 Nickelodeon declined  an option to renew Doug for a new season  despite Jenkins being signed for 65  episodes the decision which reportedly  came as a shock was based upon some  budget tightening underway at  Nickelodeon  however Nickelodeon's voluntary loss was  Disney's gain as it allowed the House of  mouse to scoop up the production rights  and studio for Doug and proceed with  carrying the hit show forward the show  was retooled as brand spanking new Doug  as part of the ABC Saturday morning  cartoon lineup for three additional  seasons and spawned a live-action  musical a line of toy merchandise with  fast-food cross-marketing and even a  film called Doug's first movie uh not so  happy ending the saga of Doug funnie and  Patti mayonnaise never quite came to a  close  leaving fans to wonder whether the green  vested gentlemen managed to win Patti's  heart for the long haul according to  Jenkins the answer is likely no he told  Entertainment Weekly that he couldn't  picture the two ending up together as  adults because it's just not realistic  saying most people don't end up with  their first love perhaps Jenkins is  merely jaded based on his own experience  later in life with the real-life person  who inspired Patti in a Nick animation  podcast he recounted his reunion with a  woman in question and admitted that they  didn't enjoy some sudden romance  despite his hopes that it might happen  after she reached out to invite him to  dinner Jenkins admitted to wondering if  something might spark until she said oh  oh I want you to meet my husband sorry  ugh
 

